description:
Function calls where a braced expression is present in the last argument deserves a call out, as it is a special function call style (i.e. not exactly flat or expanded)

```r
map(xs, function(x) {
  do_something(x)
})

map(xs, \(x) {
  do_something(x)
})

test_that("description", {
  test_me
})
```

These make for particularly unique function call layouts, because normally the expansion of the braced expression over multiple lines would force the expansion of every argument in the function call, looking like:

```r
map(
  xs, 
  function(x) {
    do_something(x)
  }
)

map(
  xs, 
  \(x) {
    do_something(x)
  }
)

test_that(
  "description", 
  {
    test_me
  }
)
```

Note that this only applies to trailing braced expressions, for example with `tryCatch()` we do full expansion of every argument

```r
tryCatch(
  {
    expr
  },
  error = function(e) {}
)
```

We get close to making this statement in https://style.tidyverse.org/syntax.html#control-flow but don't quite spell it out. I'd like to take a stand on forcing `else` to show up right after `}` if the `if` branch is "embraced":


```{r .bad}
foo <- function(x) {
  if (TRUE) {
    return(1)
  }
  else {
    return(2)
  }
}
```

IIUC this only parses in the first place because it's in a sub-expression. Better would be:

```{r .good}
foo <- function(x) {
  if (TRUE) {
    return(1)
  } else {
    return(2)
  }
}
```

FWIW `styler` already implemented a change that would fix this by default: https://github.com/r-lib/styler/issues/792

It seems as capitalization is inconsistent for these words in the code examples, e.g. compare [functions](https://style.tidyverse.org/functions.html#return) and [error messages](https://style.tidyverse.org/error-messages.html). Also, as other naming conventions in this style guide are to use `snake_case`, one might even think of all lower case:

```r
# good
if (x) {
  return(FALSE)
}
# bad
if (x) return(FALSE)
```

cf http://style.tidyverse.org/functions.html#return

I think it's good practice to put it on a newline so it's not hidden behind the if condition:

```r
# Good
find_abs <- function(x, y) {
  if (x > 0) {
    return(x)
  }
  x * -1
}

# Bad
find_abs <- function(x, y) {
  if (x > 0) return(x)
  x * -1
}
```

Seems like there is inconsistent spacing after `function(x, y)` within  the `# Good` example for [https://github.com/tidyverse/style/blob/master/03-functions.Rmd#return](url)

```
# Good
find_abs <- function(x, y){
  if (x > 0) return(x)
  x * -1
}
add_two <- function(x, y) {
  x + y
}
```

Should `find_abs <- function(x, y){` be replaced with `find_abs <- function(x, y) {`?
